[Fact:spectrumcomputing:Spectrum Computing]
<a href="https://spectrumcomputing.co.uk/entry/7173"
  rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">Warlord</a>
(credits, magazine scores, compilations and the published solution).

[Fact:story:Celts Against Rome]
Sinclair User set the scene: Vespasian's legions have fought their way up to
the Scottish border, where they are stopped by a tribe led by you, the
warlord. The gods agree to settle the war by single combat: you and a Roman
centurion are sent into a Celtic Otherworld, and the first back to the real
world wins. See the <a href="https://spectrumcomputing.co.uk/zxsr.php?id=7173"
  rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">reviews</a>.

[Fact:staff:Solved Without Knowing]
Computer &amp; Video Games noticed that the game lets you solve a puzzle
without telling you. The first time through the enchanted wood the path was
easy, but without the staff "some very strange things began to happen". See
the <a href="https://spectrumcomputing.co.uk/zxsr.php?id=7173"
  rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">reviews</a>.

[Fact:tie:No Tying]
Sinclair User found it odd that the game didn't understand "tie" while you
were holding a rope, although the game does have the message "You've nothing
to tie him with." at #R$E091, which is printed only if you capture the Roman
without the rope (#R$F544). See the <a href="https://spectrumcomputing.co.uk/zxsr.php?id=7173"
  rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">reviews</a>.

[Fact:guild:The Guild]
Warlord was reissued on The Guild compilation in 1986 with Border Harrier
and The Golden Cobra, and later on the Your Sinclair issue 48 Smash Tape
Special Edition. Your Sinclair issue 53 printed a solution. Source:
<a href="https://spectrumcomputing.co.uk/entry/7173"
  rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">Spectrum Computing</a>.

[Fact:ending:The Last Ring]
Stepping into a ring is handled at #R$F5F9. In the Crystal Cavern it takes
you to room #N$57; anywhere else it takes you to room #N($43), unless bit 5 of
#R$A76D is set, in which case it takes you to room #N($56), which is the end of
the game, written as a room description. That bit is set when the urn is
thrown into the fire at #R$F4B9, which also kills the Fomorian tribe.

[Fact:staff-copy:A Spare Staff]
The routine at #R$F0AC does the same as the "get staff" code at #R$F7E9
(the same exit changes, in a different order) and is never used.

[Fact:vocab:Get Or Take]
Warlord's vocabulary at #R$E0B1 accepts GET as well as TAKE. Its successor
#SHOCK$B0ED(After Shock) only knows TAKE, which several reviewers complained
about.

[Fact:swearing:Don't Be Disgusting]
Token 25 (#N$19) in the vocabulary, at #R$E0B1(#N$E15B), holds four words,
stored as "FUCK", "SCRE", "RAPE" and "STUF" (the game only keeps the first
four letters, so SCRE is SCREW and STUF is STUFF). Use one of them on
something and #R$FBB9 answers with a flat "Don't be disgusting." (#R$DBF4).
Unlike #JEWELS$FB83(The Jewels Of Babylon), it doesn't keep count.
